  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trinidad and Tobago has a mechanism to collect wage data at specified intervals that improves the availability of data on average wages, the wage share, or wage inequality, or other new wage indicators</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trinidad and Tobago&#x2019;s statistical system faces fragmentation and resource limitations. The Central Statistical Office (CSO), operating under the Ministry of Planning and National Development, serves as the technical entity responsible for data collection and statistical system coordination. In close collaboration with Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean (ECLAC), World Health Organization (WHO)/ Pan American Health Organization (PAHO), and United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), the ILO has been actively engaged with the CSO to enhance regular statistical production.

Furthermore, the Ministry of Labour has a while ago instituted a Labour Market Information System (LMIS). However, the system&#x2019;s effectiveness is impeded by constraints within the statistical ecosystem, as previously identified. Therefore, it is crucial to strengthen the LMIS unit&#x2019;s capacity and foster collaboration between the CSO and the LMIS unit.

This CPO aims to strengthen CSO in conducting and digitalising regular labour force surveys and population and housing census and capacitating the staff in government on labour market statistics.</narrative>
  </description>
